        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory (Path Traversal)",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Path Traversal in File Loading  [CVSS 7.5: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N]"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "scripts/replay/replay.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 34
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "https://github.com/logic-gambit/marketdata-bhvaik/blob/main/scripts/replay/replay.js#L34"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "severity": "high",
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "cvssRating": "High",
            "category": "CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory (Path Traversal)",
            "cvssVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N",
            "cwe": "CWE-22 - Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory (Path Traversal)",
            "cweId": "CWE-22",
            "cweName": "Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory (Path Traversal)",
            "cvssScore": 7.5,
            "offensivePriority": "P3",
            "offensivePriorityLabel": "Internal Network / Privileged Position",
            "offensivePriorityReason": "Internal Network / Privileged Position | *exposure unverified \u2014 no CMDB context; AV:N (network-routable; internet exposure unconfirmed)*",
            "confidence": 0.9,
            "description": "#### Description\nIn the handleWebSocketMessage function, a 'LOAD' command can specify a filename that is directly appended to the './logs/' path without validation or sanitization. This direct concatenation can be manipulated to traverse directories and read unintended files.\n\n#### Impact\nAn attacker can exploit a path traversal vulnerability to read arbitrary files within the context of the application, as the input filename is directly concatenated into the path without validation. This can lead to information disclosure, affecting the confidentiality of the system.\n\n#### Exploit scenario\nAn attacker sends a WebSocket message with the payload 'LOAD ../../etc/passwd'. The application loads the specified file path './logs/../../etc/passwd', which allows the attacker to disclose sensitive files such as /etc/passwd.\n\n#### Preconditions\n- Attacker has access to send WebSocket messages to the application\n\n```\nfileReader = ReplayFileReader.for(`./logs/${filename}`);\n```\n\n#### How to fix\nImplement a validation mechanism in handleWebSocketMessage to sanitize the filename, ensuring it does not contain any path traversal sequences like '../'.\n\n**Exploitability:** Allows arbitrary file reading due to lack of path validation. No design controls mitigate this.\n\n#### Adversarial verification\n**Verdict:** TRUE_POSITIVE (confidence: 9/10) \u2014 Lack of input sanitization leads to path traversal vulnerability.\n\nThe reported finding suggests a path traversal vulnerability where an attacker can manipulate the filename in a WebSocket message to load unintended files. After reviewing the relevant code, the following details are noted:\n\n1. **Entry Point Verification**: \n   - The `handleWebSocketMessage` function in `scripts/replay/replay.js` is externally reachable and processes WebSocket messages. This function uses a regex to parse received messages and extract the command and filename.\n\n2. **Path Construction and Validation**:\n   - In `handleWebSocketMessage`, the `LOAD` command constructs a file path using `ReplayFileReader.for(`./logs/${filename}`)`. The file path is directly concatenated with the user-supplied filename.\n   - The `ReplayFileReader` class in `scripts/replay/common/ReplayFileReader.js` has a method `checkFile` that merely checks for the existence of the file using `fs.existsSync`, without validating or sanitizing the file path against traversal patterns.\n\n3. **Absence of Input Sanitization and Reflection**:\n   - There's no evidence of input validation or mitigation measures to sanitize paths, such as removal of traversal patterns (`..`), within the investigated files. Hence, paths like `../../etc/passwd` could potentially be resolved and accessed.\n\n4. **Impact Validation**:\n   - The vulnerability indeed aligns with the typical characteristics of directory traversal, allowing an attacker with the capability of sending crafted WebSocket messages to read arbitrary files on the server.\n\nGiven the above findings, there is a valid path traversal vulnerability without mitigations preventing exploitation.",
            "findingId": "57b9c8-finding-scripts/replay/replay.js-34",
            "remediationCommand": "cd /Users/bhavikpanchal/Desktop/orgScanTest/GambitScanner && source /Users/bhavikpanchal/Desktop/orgScanTest/GambitScanner/venv/bin/activate && export GAMBITSCA_SCAN_ID=\"2026-10-21T16:04:38Z__marketdata-bhvaik\" && gambitsca remediate --repo /Users/bhavikpanchal/Desktop/orgScanTest/GambitScanner/batch-workspace/marketdata-bhvaik --finding \"57b9c8-finding-scripts/replay/replay.js-34\" --validate --config /Users/bhavikpanchal/Desktop/orgScanTest/GambitScanner/config.yaml"
